 "result": "The default session turns those types into tagged JSON and back using a tag-based serializer.\n\n- **`TypedCookieSerializer`** (`src/tessel/json/tag.py:219`) holds the registered tags.\n  - `dumps()` and `loads()` call `tag()` and `untag()`.\n  - On the way out, `tag()` tries each tag in order and wraps the first matching value as a one-key dict.\n  - On the way in, `untag()` looks up that key and reverses the wrapping.\n- **`TypeMarker`** (`tag.py:60`) is the base class for tags. Each subclass has a key and decides which values it handles. It also converts a value to JSON-safe form and back.\n- **Built-in tags**, all in `tag.py`:\n  - `TagTuple` (line 133) handles tuples.\n  - `TagBytes` (line 159) handles bytes.\n  - `TagDateTime` (line 205) handles datetimes.\n  - `TagDict`, `PassDict`, `PassList`, `TagMarkup` and `TagUUID` handle dicts, lists, markup and UUIDs.\n- **Default session wiring** (`src/tessel/sessions.py`):\n  - `session_json_serializer = TypedCookieSerializer()` (line 273) is the default serializer instance.\n  - `SignedCookieSessionInterface` (line 284) uses it. It builds a `URLSafeTimedSerializer` (line 313) that signs the cookie.\n  - `SignedCookieSession` (line 57) is the session object that gets stored.\n\nI located these by searching class and method names. I didn't read the method bodies or the lines where the serializer is passed in, so the order of the tags and the exact dict key each one uses are unchecked.",
